## Why This Is Asked
This question tests deep production debugging skills, including multi-tenant cache management, Redis eviction policies, and safe rollout procedures under traffic.

## Key Concepts
- Redis eviction policies (volatile-lru, allkeys-lru, etc.) and TTL management
- Multi-tenant namespace isolation in Redis using key prefixes
- Canary rollout strategies (e.g., Argo Rollouts or manual phased deployments)
- Observability: Redis INFO metrics, hit/miss ratios, and application latency metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Verify current eviction policy
const evictionPolicy = await redis.configGet('maxmemory-policy');
console.log('Current eviction policy:', evictionPolicy);

// Check keyspace stats
const info = await redis.info('stats');
const hits = info.match(/keyspace_hits:(\d+)/)[1];
const misses = info.match(/keyspace_misses:(\d+)/)[1];
const hitRate = hits / (hits + misses);
console.log('Hit rate:', hitRate);
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests distributed-systems thinking: exactly-once, dedup, backpressure, observability.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once processing
- Deduplication with dedup_id
- Backpressure strategies (batching, pause vs drop)
- Observability (tracing, metrics, logs)
- Kafka vs Kinesis trade-offs
- Idempotent sinks and compensating actions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for idempotent sink write
function writeEvent(evt, sink, store) {
  const id = evt.dedup_id;
  if (store.seen(id)) return;
  sink.write(evt);
  store.markSeen(id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test guarantees for partial failures?
- How would you ensure exactly-once with at-least-once queues?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to diagnose production rollouts with service meshes and canaries, focusing on end-to-end validation, live traffic checks, and safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Canary/rollout health in Istio
- Rollout status and events
- Readiness/Liveness probes relevance
- Safe rollback without downtime
- Synthetic traffic validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal helper to parse rollout JSON and extract canary status (pseudo, for illustration)
function getCanaryStatus(rolloutJson) {
  try {
    const r = JSON.parse(rolloutJson);
    return r?.status?.canary?.traffic || 0;
  } catch {
    return 0;
  }
}
```

